Bring everything to one side and use distributivity of the dot product: \(\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b} = \vec{a}\cdot\vec{c}\) gives \(\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b} - \vec{a}\cdot\vec{c} = 0\), that is \(\vec{a}\cdot(\vec{b} - \vec{c}) = 0\). A dot product vanishes only if one of the two vectors is the zero vector or the two are perpendicular. Here \(\vec{a} 
e \vec{0}\) is given, and the vectors are stated to be unequal so \(\vec{b} 
e \vec{c}\), which makes \(\vec{b} - \vec{c} 
e \vec{0}\). With both vectors non-zero, the only remaining possibility is perpendicularity. Hence the angle between \(\vec{a}\) and \(\vec{b} - \vec{c}\) is \( rac{\pi}{2}\), i.e. \(90^{\circ}\).
